 * decisions; see that file and docs/implementation/pdf-security-review/00-summary.md for the
 * review itself). This file exists so PdfSafeExceptions::isApproved() -- and, through it,
 * Fields::datasheet_valid() -- can apply those SAME approvals at runtime, in local, staging
 * AND production, without the migration-only source tree (Sources::safe(), Storage::guard(),
 * Planner, docs/, /legacy) ever needing to be deployed. This is the PORTABLE half of the
 * design; migration/PdfApprovals.php (resolution/audit against the full review, including
 * Group A sanitized substitutes that still need the local sanitized-file tree) is the
 * migration-only half and is never used at runtime -- see PdfApprovals.php's own docblock.
 *
 * Group A (sanitized substitutes) is deliberately NOT reconciled here: a Group A attachment
 * already passes Media::valid() on its own, sanitized-byte merits at runtime -- it never
 * needs an approval lookup outside the one-time migration write. Only group_b_exception
 * entries (a PDF whose ORIGINAL bytes stay attached as-is, approved as a structurally
 * verified false positive or a benign, inert internal action) are relevant here.
 *
 * Never inferred, never regenerated by pattern: every entry is a literal copy of an
 * already-approved group_b_exception record. Adding one means a human approved a NEW PDF
 * through that same security review process and copied the resulting record here -- this
 * file is not a place to invent, guess, or widen an approval.
 *
 * Keyed by `legacy_path` (the file's original relative path in the legacy source tree) --
 * NEVER by attachment_id or any other environment-specific WordPress ID, since those are
 * auto-increment and differ between local/staging/production (same portability rationale as
 * legacy-url-map.php's entity_key convention -- see that file's own docblock).
 *
 * Each entry:
 *  - source_sha256: the EXACT approved file content hash. A current attachment's on-disk
 *    bytes must match this exactly (hash_equals) -- any other content, even at the same
 *    legacy_path, is never covered.
 *  - classification / reason_code / reason: the human review's own record, carried verbatim
 *    for audit -- the security gate itself is legacy_path + source_sha256 matching exactly,
 *    never the classification/reason text, but these are kept so this file can stand alone
 *    as the approval's own evidence without the source review docs being deployed.
 *  - products: the SQL product id(s) this PDF was reviewed for, carried verbatim for audit;
 *    an empty list means the file had no documented owning product at review time. */
